1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,262
Welcome to Module Eighteen.

2
00:00:02,262 --> 00:00:05,387
A young songbird is not born knowing its song.

3
00:00:05,387 --> 00:00:08,200
It listens to a tutor and stores a template.

4
00:00:08,200 --> 00:00:15,350
Then it practises, badly at first, a scrappy subsong, and keeps adjusting until what it sings matches what it remembers.

5
00:00:15,350 --> 00:00:17,800
The finished song is a learned behaviour.

6
00:00:17,800 --> 00:00:24,250
It can be passed on, it can vary, and over generations, the versions that get copied spread into local dialects.

7
00:00:24,250 --> 00:00:26,762
Agents now have learned behaviours too.

8
00:00:26,762 --> 00:00:28,412
They're called skills.

9
00:00:28,412 --> 00:00:33,662
A skill is a small folder with one key file in it, called SKILL dot M D.

10
00:00:33,662 --> 00:00:37,762
At the top, a name and a short description of when to use it.

11
00:00:37,762 --> 00:00:43,675
Below that, the instructions: a checklist, some rules, and the exact format of the result.

12
00:00:43,675 --> 00:00:47,112
It can also carry extra reference files and scripts.

13
00:00:47,112 --> 00:00:50,875
The trick that makes skills work is called progressive disclosure.

14
00:00:50,875 --> 00:00:54,950
The agent always sees the short description of every skill it has.

15
00:00:54,950 --> 00:01:01,775
But the full instructions only load when the skill is actually used, and the extra files load only if they're needed.

16
00:01:01,775 --> 00:01:07,587
So you can keep a large library of skills, and it costs almost nothing until the moment one is called.

17
00:01:07,587 --> 00:01:14,962
A recent study of eleven coding agents found that this skill format is now the most widely adopted way to extend them.

18
00:01:14,962 --> 00:01:16,850
You can write skills yourself.

19
00:01:16,850 --> 00:01:18,312
That's tutoring.

20
00:01:18,312 --> 00:01:22,937
And now, agents are starting to write and rewrite their own, from experience.

21
00:01:22,937 --> 00:01:29,112
The CMU course describes research where an agent turns its successful attempts into reusable workflows.

22
00:01:29,112 --> 00:01:31,212
But notice one detail.

23
00:01:31,212 --> 00:01:37,287
Before saving a workflow, the researchers ask a judge to check whether the attempt really succeeded.

24
00:01:37,287 --> 00:01:42,137
Because a remembered mistake will steer the agent wrong, again and again.

25
00:01:42,137 --> 00:01:45,687
That's exactly where the songbird metaphor breaks.

26
00:01:45,687 --> 00:01:51,525
A bird checks its song against a template, and a population sorts good versions from bad ones.

27
00:01:51,525 --> 00:01:57,562
An agent that rewrites its own skills has no template and no population, unless you give it one.

28
00:01:57,562 --> 00:02:03,162
It can improve a skill into something worse, and then use that worse version every single time.

29
00:02:03,162 --> 00:02:06,062
Cultural drift, with no selection at all.

30
00:02:06,062 --> 00:02:09,175
So in the lab, you'll supply the selection.

31
00:02:09,175 --> 00:02:19,075
You'll write a field data quality skill for your capstone, with your own hard-won rules in it, including this one: a zero count is an observation, never drop it.

32
00:02:19,075 --> 00:02:26,287
Your agent will plant known problems in some messy spreadsheets, and then you'll test the skill the way you'd test any intervention.

33
00:02:26,287 --> 00:02:28,400
With the skill, and without it.

34
00:02:28,400 --> 00:02:34,950
Then you'll let the agent rewrite its own skill, and check both versions on a held-out file that the rewrite never saw.

35
00:02:34,950 --> 00:02:41,150
If the new version only wins on the files it was tuned on, you've just watched overfitting happen in front of you.

36
00:02:41,150 --> 00:02:44,362
Open the reading, and let's teach your agent a song.

